AMDGPU LTE fork: build and module parameters
============================================

The default branch, releases/amd-31.60-lte, contains AMDGPU timeout/recovery,
SMU power-policy and diagnostic changes, plus experimental cgroup-owned GPU
sandbox controls. The sandbox implementation is in driver commit af3f47b9d;
its live container/GPU validation remains outstanding. Controls default off.
The upstream Linux README is retained below.

Build the packaged AMDGPU modules
--------------------------------

This repository is a full kernel source tree. Running make at its root builds
a kernel; the tested out-of-tree driver workflow instead overlays the changed
sources onto AMD's packaged amdgpu-dkms source tree. Do not copy the whole
AMD directory over the package: the package has its own compatibility/build
layout.

The companion Pearl checkout contains the checksum-pinned source stager,
DKMS installer/rollback workflow, host helper and validation fixtures at
``tools/gpu-sandbox/``. Those tools are in Pearl, not in this repository.
Use a Pearl checkout containing the experimental helper commits, whose
``driver/manifest.json`` pins driver commit
``af3f47b9d355dc51fc901c193ea25f3fe4e84c47``. The separate production installer
under ``miner/pearlhash-native/tools/amdgpu/`` pins the earlier non-sandbox
revision; it does not install the experimental sandbox driver.

Prerequisites: packaged source ``amdgpu-7.1.9-2407772.24.04``, matching target
kernel headers, GCC/build tools, Git and Python 3; DKMS is additionally needed
for installation. The target external-module build passed on x86_64 with
``7.0.0-42-generic``. Other kernels need their own build and runtime checks.

Build as a normal user, using a new source output directory::

    PEARL_CHECKOUT=/path/to/pearl
    python3 "$PEARL_CHECKOUT/tools/gpu-sandbox/driver/overlay.py" stage \
      --stock /usr/src/amdgpu-7.1.9-2407772.24.04 \
      --output /tmp/amdgpu-lte-source &&
    amdgpu_build_dir=$(mktemp -d /tmp/amdgpu-lte-build.XXXXXX) &&
    cp -a /tmp/amdgpu-lte-source/. "$amdgpu_build_dir/" &&
    (cd "$amdgpu_build_dir" && make KERNELVER="$(uname -r)" num_cpu_cores=8)

The stager rejects existing output directories and verifies the packaged-base
and resulting source hashes. Build a disposable copy because the vendor
pre-build step rewrites CONFIG names and generates compatibility headers.
Building does not install a driver, update initramfs, load a module or start
the helper. Successful compilation does not establish hardware stability.

For installation, first inspect ``dkms status -m amdgpu`` and choose an already
cached rollback version for the same kernel/architecture. Preview the explicit
experimental workflow as your normal user::

    python3 "$PEARL_CHECKOUT/tools/gpu-sandbox/driver/dkms.py" install \
      --development --dry-run --restore-version YOUR_CACHED_DKMS_VERSION

Review Pearl's ``tools/gpu-sandbox/driver/README.md`` for the live install,
Secure Boot/signing, loaded-module verification and rollback procedure. Keep
the exact reviewed bundle and its rollback record. The installer does not
load/unload the GPU driver, reboot, set module options or start the service;
the experimental DKMS version has AUTOINSTALL=no.

Fork-specific AMDGPU parameters
-------------------------------

These are read-only module-load parameters. Kernel command-line spelling is
``amdgpu.NAME=VALUE``; modprobe configuration uses ``options amdgpu NAME=VALUE``.
Apply changes at a controlled boot after installing the intended module;
refresh the target initramfs when needed by the distribution's boot setup.

* ``lte_debug=0`` (default): diagnostic trace mask. Bits 1=SMU, 2=PM and
  4=PPT; 7 enables all three. This enables trace instrumentation, not a reset
  workaround. ``tools/gpu/amdgpu-lte-capture.sh`` captures the fork's traces.

* ``smu_etime_streak=3`` (default): consecutive SMU response timeouts before
  fail-fast behavior. 0 disables streak-based timeout escalation. This does
  not set the GPU scheduler timeout or disable normal GPU recovery.

* ``min_power_cap=0`` (default): retain the SKU minimum PPT floor. A positive
  value in watts requests a lower PPT0 floor on discrete Navi 48 only
  (GC 12.0.1, MP1 14.0.2/14.0.3); APUs, SR-IOV VFs, other IPs and PPT1 retain
  native policy. For example, 170 permits requesting 180 W when it was below
  the original floor. It does not set the actual power cap or raise its upper
  bound. Operation below the SKU floor is experimental and may hang the GPU.

* ``sandbox=0`` (default): no sandbox control device. Explicit ``sandbox=1``
  enables the privileged versioned control ABI at ``/dev/amdgpu-sandbox``.
  The connected feature mask is 511; this reports availability, not completed
  hardware validation. The Pearl host helper is required to create policies
  and gate container launch. This is shared-GPU runaway containment, without
  hostile-tenant hard isolation or a hard GPU-time quota.

Example diagnostic/power-floor kernel command line::

    amdgpu.lte_debug=7 amdgpu.smu_etime_streak=3 amdgpu.min_power_cap=170

Equivalent modprobe configuration line::

    options amdgpu lte_debug=7 smu_etime_streak=3 min_power_cap=170

For controlled sandbox validation, additionally opt in with
``amdgpu.sandbox=1`` on the kernel command line (or ``sandbox=1`` on the
options line). Installation alone does not enable it.

After boot, compare the module on disk with the loaded source identity and
check the effective parameters::

    modinfo -n amdgpu
    modinfo -F srcversion amdgpu
    cat /sys/module/amdgpu/srcversion
    cat /sys/module/amdgpu/parameters/lte_debug
    cat /sys/module/amdgpu/parameters/smu_etime_streak
    cat /sys/module/amdgpu/parameters/min_power_cap
    cat /sys/module/amdgpu/parameters/sandbox

The two srcversion values should match the verified candidate. ``modinfo``
alone describes the disk module; absent parameters can indicate an older
loaded driver. ``modinfo -p amdgpu`` lists all supported module parameters.
For a 170 W floor, the selected GPU's ``power1_cap_min`` should report
170000000 microwatts; verify the actual cap separately through ``power1_cap``.
Locate the hwmon directory by the selected PCI device, rather than assuming
a fixed hwmon index.


Container GPU sandbox support (experimental)
-------------------------------------------

The driver and Pearl helper implement shared-GPU containment of accidental
runaway headless graphics and ROCm/HIP workloads. Supported source paths are
rootful Docker, rootful Podman and rootless Podman. Rootless Docker is deferred.
Real runtime startup, cgroup/BPF behavior and GPU fault/recovery tests remain
unverified; the feature mask is not a statement that those tests passed.

Architecture and requirements
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

* Enable ``amdgpu.sandbox=1`` at a controlled boot of the reviewed driver.
  The root-only control device uses a versioned ABI and requires CAP_SYS_ADMIN
  in the initial user namespace. Containers do not receive this control node.

* Run the root-owned Pearl helper service on a unified cgroup v2 host with
  memory, pids and dmem controllers, writable delegation and cgroup device
  BPF support. The supplied systemd unit uses DelegateSubgroup=daemon and
  preserves workload cgroups on helper restart. Host RAM/swap/process limits
  and the driver policy are configured outside the payload's control.

* A root daemon manages all three runtime paths. Rootful launches require a
  root caller. Rootless Podman requires an explicitly allowed host UID in
  /etc/default/gpu-sandbox, a usable user namespace/runtime setup, existing
  device permissions and /usr/bin/crun. Its image must exist in that user's
  image store. Rootful Podman and Docker use their respective stores.

* Launch through ``gpu-sandbox run``. The helper creates the policy/device
  restrictions, forces a trusted static gate as the container entrypoint,
  verifies the init PID and final cgroup/GPU footprint, then authenticates
  payload release. A missing capability, failed readback or missing gate
  prevents release. An ordinary ``docker run --device ...`` does not create
  a sandbox policy or provide this launch gate.

* The device filter allows basic container character devices, the selected
  render node and, with ``--compute``, the discovered KFD node. KFD's major
  number is dynamic. Driver ownership checks constrain use of that shared
  KFD interface to the selected GPU. Passing the whole /dev/dri directory or
  enabling privileged containers is not part of the helper interface.

Policy ownership and resource controls
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Each policy references one cgroup subtree and one PCI GPU, with immutable
limits and identity. Nested or overlapping policies are rejected. Existing
clients/allocations retain their captured owner across kernel workers and
process movement; moving a caller does not transfer its old objects into a
new budget. DRM/KFD descriptor boundaries and DMA-BUF/KFD IPC imports reject
foreign or unmanaged owners; sharing within the same policy is supported.

The connected driver features are OWNERSHIP, BO, VRAM, PINNED, CONTEXTS,
LEGACY, USERQ, KFD and FAULTS (combined mask 511). Their controls cover:

* VRAM physical residency; total BO bytes and BO/count metadata, including
  owned VM page tables and private KFD/MES queue/process allocations.
* Per-mm union reservations for overlapping userptr/SVM ranges. This is a
  conservative virtual reservation budget, not an exact pinned-RSS meter.
  Registration and KFD import-alias metadata also consume BO units.
* Client roots and DRM contexts, DRM/KFD direct queues, and outstanding
  legacy scheduler jobs. A direct queue is not a GPU-time reservation.
* Host RAM, swap and process count through cgroup v2; native dmem VRAM limits
  plus captured-owner driver VRAM accounting for worker allocations.

Defaults are 4096 BO units, 64 context units, 64 direct queues, 256 outstanding
legacy jobs and 512 processes. Client roots also consume context units. Byte
budgets are explicit and page aligned; the example below uses 2 GiB VRAM,
4 GiB BO bytes, 1 GiB pinned reservations, 4 GiB RAM and no swap. Replace the
PCI address with the selected GPU's full lowercase BDF::

    {
      "gpu": "0000:0b:00.0",
      "mode": "observe",
      "vram_bytes": 2147483648,
      "buffer_bytes": 4294967296,
      "pinned_bytes": 1073741824,
      "ram_bytes": 4294967296,
      "swap_bytes": 0,
      "max_bos": 4096,
      "max_contexts": 64,
      "max_queues": 64,
      "max_jobs": 256,
      "max_pids": 512,
      "fault_threshold": 3,
      "fault_window_ms": 60000
    }

``observe`` is the default: record predicted limit violations, keep helper
memory/dmem/process limits unlimited, and do not automatically terminate a
workload for fault/device-loss events. Device restrictions, ownership checks,
the gate and explicit operator stop still apply. ``enforce`` applies numeric
limits and rejects admissions exceeding driver budgets. Change the policy's
mode explicitly to opt in; a new workload/policy is needed to change limits.

Quarantine, recovery and helper restart
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

In enforce mode, the default threshold is three distinct attributable GPU
incidents in 60 seconds. Fence/queue identities deduplicate repeated reports;
unknown ownership does not justify terminating another policy. Quarantine
blocks new objects/submissions and queue restore, with targeted DRM/KFD queue
eviction and retry on incomplete removal. The host helper sends TERM and,
after at most two seconds of grace, uses cgroup.kill on that policy subtree.
Explicit ``stop`` requests the same quarantine/termination in either mode.

The targeted helper path does not request a whole-GPU reset. Normal driver
recovery remains available and may still affect clients sharing the GPU.
Existing BOs can be relocated during quarantine within the VRAM ceiling;
TTM can fall back to GTT. Temporary source and destination residency both
count, so a VRAM-to-VRAM move can require spare budget.

Kernel policies/counters and attached device restrictions survive daemon
failure. A durable host journal and authoritative kernel inventory support
restart reconciliation and event-gap detection. Quarantine is irreversible
for a policy; restarting work requires a new container/policy. Policy retirement
waits for the empty cgroup to be removed and outstanding driver references to
be released.

Controlled usage and validation
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Build/review/install the helper bundle using Pearl's
``tools/gpu-sandbox/README.md`` and the driver workflow above. Its installer
neither enables nor starts the service. After the controlled driver boot,
run ``sudo gpu-sandbox doctor``; it checks ABI/controller availability, not
GPU isolation or runtime correctness. The isolated privileged cgroup/BPF test
is documented in the helper README and should precede container/GPU tests.

Save the example as policy.json and check its schema/defaults as a normal
user::

    gpu-sandbox validate-policy policy.json

After service configuration/start, example rootful Podman graphics and
Docker HIP launches are below. The reviewed image must contain the compiled
fixtures, compatible GPU userspace libraries and /usr/bin/timeout::

    sudo gpu-sandbox run --runtime podman --policy policy.json \
      --image YOUR_REVIEWED_IMAGE -- \
      /usr/bin/timeout 90 /opt/fixtures/vulkan-smoke --run-seconds 60

    sudo gpu-sandbox run --runtime docker --compute --policy policy.json \
      --image YOUR_REVIEWED_IMAGE -- \
      /usr/bin/timeout 90 /opt/fixtures/hip-smoke --run-seconds 60

For rootless Podman, run as the allowed non-root host user, use
``--runtime rootless-podman`` without sudo, and use that user's image store.
Add ``--compute`` for HIP. ``run`` returns launch metadata, not the payload's
completion status; capture runtime logs/wait results using the returned exact
container ID while it is active, before automatic finished-container cleanup.
Operator commands use the returned sandbox name::

    sudo gpu-sandbox status --name SANDBOX_NAME
    sudo gpu-sandbox events --name SANDBOX_NAME
    sudo gpu-sandbox stop SANDBOX_NAME

Rootless owners use these commands without sudo for their own sandboxes.
Pearl's ``tools/gpu-sandbox/fixtures/README.md`` documents bounded graphics/HIP
readback payloads and active concurrency/restart checks. Its
``VERIFICATION.md`` and ``PREPUSH-AUDIT-2026-11-06.md`` record the remaining
live cgroup, runtime, sharing, fault, recovery and multi-client test gates.

This does not provide hostile-tenant hard isolation, dedicated hardware
partitions, a hard GPU-time quota, or assurance that an unrecoverable hardware
hang cannot affect the host. Container/GPU execution and the complete live
containment behavior have not been validated in this implementation session.


Linux kernel
============

The Linux kernel is the core of any Linux operating system. It manages hardware,
system resources, and provides the fundamental services for all other software.

Quick Start
-----------

* Report a bug: See Documentation/admin-guide/reporting-issues.rst
* Get the latest kernel: https://kernel.org
* Build the kernel: See Documentation/admin-guide/quickly-build-trimmed-linux.rst
* Join the community: https://lore.kernel.org/

Essential Documentation
-----------------------

All users should be familiar with:

* Building requirements: Documentation/process/changes.rst
* Code of Conduct: Documentation/process/code-of-conduct.rst
* License: See COPYING

Documentation can be built with make htmldocs or viewed online at:
https://www.kernel.org/doc/html/latest/


Who Are You?
============

Find your role below:

* New Kernel Developer - Getting started with kernel development
* Academic Researcher - Studying kernel internals and architecture
* Security Expert - Hardening and vulnerability analysis
* Backport/Maintenance Engineer - Maintaining stable kernels
* System Administrator - Configuring and troubleshooting
* Maintainer - Leading subsystems and reviewing patches
* Hardware Vendor - Writing drivers for new hardware
* Distribution Maintainer - Packaging kernels for distros
* AI Coding Assistant - LLMs and AI-powered development tools


